%[text] # Calculus - Integrals
%[text] ## Information
%[text] This curriculum module contains interactive [MATLAB® live scripts](https://www.mathworks.com/products/matlab/live-editor.html) that teach fundamental concepts and basic terminology related to integral calculus. There is a focus on numerical approximation and graphical representation as tools for understanding the concepts of integral calculus.
%[text] ### Background
%[text] You can use these [live scripts](internal:H_6A85AE0A) as demonstrations in lectures, class activities, or interactive assignments outside of class. Calculus - Integrals covers Riemann sum approximations to definite integrals, indefinite integrals as antiderivatives, and the fundamental theorem of calculus. It also covers the indefinite integrals of powers, exponentials, natural logarithms, sines, and cosines as well as substitution and integration by parts. Applications include area and power. In addition to the full scripts, visualizations, and practice scripts there is a [Calculus Flashcards app](internal:H_1F9459BC) included as well.
%[text] The instructions inside the live scripts will guide you through the exercises and activities. Get started with each live script by running it one section at a time. To stop running the script or a section midway (for example, when an animation is in progress), use the ![Stop icon](text:image:8e30) Stop button in the **RUN** section of the **Live Editor** tab in the MATLAB Toolstrip.
%[text] Looking for more? Find an issue? Have a suggestion? Please contact the [MathWorks Educator Content Development team](https://www.mathworks.com/matlabcentral/profile/authors/37969341).
%[text] 
%[text] ### Contact Us
%[text] Contact the [MathWorks Educator Content Development Team](mailto:onlineteaching@mathworks.com) if you would like to request assistance, provide feedback, or if you have a question.
%[text] 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_30BC7141] ### Prerequisites
%[text] This module assumes a knowledge of functions that is standard in precalculus course materials regarding powers, exponentials, absolute values, logarithms, sines, cosines, rational functions, and asymptotes. It also assumes knowledge of basic area formulas, including the area of a trapezoid. With the exception of `Riemann.m` and `RiemannViz.m`, the scripts are written to follow [Calculus-Derivatives](https://www.mathworks.com/matlabcentral/fileexchange/99249-calculus-derivatives) and expect basic understanding of derivatives and derivative rules. There is little expectation of familiarity with MATLAB, but you could use [MATLAB Onramp](https://matlabacademy.mathworks.com/details/matlab-onramp/gettingstarted) as another resource to acquire familiarity with MATLAB.
%[text] 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_330E72C3] ### Getting Started
%[text] #### Accessing the Module
%[text] #### **On MATLAB Online:**
%[text] Use the [![Open in MATLAB Online Button](text:image:36d4)](https://matlab.mathworks.com/open/github/v1?repo=MathWorks-Teaching-Resources/Calculus-Integrals&project=Integrals.prj) link to download the module. You will be prompted to log in or create a MathWorks account. The project will be loaded, and you will see an app with several navigation options to get you started.
%[text] #### **On Desktop:**
%[text] Download or clone this repository. Open MATLAB, navigate to the folder containing these scripts and double-click on [Integrals.prj](<matlab: openProject("Integrals.prj")>). It will add the appropriate files to your MATLAB path and open an app that asks you where you would like to start. 
%[text] Ensure you have all the required products ([listed below](internal:H_E850B4FF)) installed. If you need to include a product, add it using the Add-On Explorer. To install an add-on, go to the **Home** tab and select  ![Add-ons icon](text:image:56e1) **Add-Ons** \> **Get Add-Ons**. 
%[text] 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_E850B4FF] ### Products
%[text] MATLAB® is used throughout. Tools from the Symbolic Math Toolbox™ are used frequently as well.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_6A85AE0A] ## Scripts
%[text:table]{"columnWidths":[95,0,94,495,98],"ignoreHeader":true}
%[text] | **Topic** | **Full Script** | **Visualizations** | <p>**Learning Goals**</p><p>In this script, students will...</p> | **Practice** |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
%[text] | [Antiderivatives](file:./Scripts/Antiderivatives.m) | <p>[Antiderivatives.m](file:./Scripts/Antiderivatives.m)</p><p>![A plot of a family of antiderivatives distinguished by different values of +C.](text:image:1293)</p> | <p>[Visualizing Antiderivatives](file:./Scripts/AntiderivativesViz.m)</p><p>[![An animated family of antiderivatives where a red curve passes through the family showing the result for different values of +C](text:image:5d09)](file:./Scripts/AntiderivativesViz.m)</p> | <p>$\\bullet${"altText":"bullet"} see a graphical presentation of the concept of general antiderivatives.</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"bullet"} develop computational fluency with the antiderivatives of powers, sines, cosines, and exponentials.</p> | <p>[Calculate Antiderivatives](file:./Scripts/AntiderivativesPractice.m)</p><p></p><p>$\\displaystyle \\int \\sin(3z)\\; dz = -\\frac{\\cos(3z)}{3}+C${"altText":"integral of sin(3z) dz = - (cos(3z))\/3 +C"}</p> |
%[text] | [Fundamental Theorem of Calculus](file:./Scripts/FundamentalTheorem.m) | <p>[FundamentalTheorem.m](file:./Scripts/FundamentalTheorem.m)</p><p>[![Area under a velocity-time curve representing the distance traveled by a skier](text:image:1353)](file:./Scripts/FundamentalTheorem.m)</p> | <p>[Visualizing the FTC](file:./Scripts/FundamentalTheoremViz.m)</p><p>[![Signed area under a sinusoidal curve between two values](text:image:0489)](file:./Scripts/FundamentalTheoremViz.m)</p> | <p>$\\bullet${"altText":"bullet"} explain the fundamental theorem of calculus.</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"bullet"} see why the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus makes sense graphically.</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"bullet"} develop computational fluency for definite integrals involving linear and rational combinations of powers, sines, cosines, exponentials and natural logarithms.</p> | <p>[Apply the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus](file:./Scripts/FundamentalTheoremPractice.m)</p><p></p><p>$\\displaystyle \\int\_1^3\\frac{1}{w^2}\\; dw = -\\frac{1}{3}+1 = \\frac{2}{3}${"altText":"integral from 1 to 3 of 1\/(w^2) dw = - 1\/3 +1 = 2\/3"}</p> |
%[text] | [Riemann Sums](file:./Scripts/Riemann.m) | <p>[Riemann.m](file:./Scripts/Riemann.m)</p><p>[![Animation of the area calculated as the sum of rectangles of steadily shrinking constant widths under a very jagged data curve](text:image:6ca0)](file:./Scripts/Riemann.m)</p> | <p>[Visualizing Riemann Sums](file:./Scripts/RiemannViz.m)</p><p>[![Area under a curve represented as a sum of many small rectangles](text:image:19c7)](file:./Scripts/RiemannViz.m)</p> | <p>$\\bullet${"altText":"bullet"} explain and apply the different approximations computed by a left-endpoint, right-endpoint, midpoint, maximum, or minimum method of selecting a height value in a Riemann sum.</p><p></p><p></p> | <p>$\\bullet${"altText":"bullet"} explain and apply the trapezoidal approximation.</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"bullet"} explain why increasing the number of intervals in an approximation will decrease the error.</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"bullet"} discuss the implications for applying calculus in applications with values that are discrete or continuous.</p> |
%[text] | [Substitution](file:./Scripts/Substitution.m) | <p>[Substitution.m](file:./Scripts/Substitution.m)</p><p>![f of flower](text:image:499d)</p> | <p>[Visualizing Substitution](file:./Scripts/SubstitutionViz.m)</p><p>[![An animation showing a negative area under a curve filled by same-size rectangles moving left followed by the same calculation made with different variables which initially moves right, then comes back left past the starting point and continues left to the end point](text:image:1273)](file:./Scripts/SubstitutionViz.m)</p> | <p>$\\bullet${"altText":"bullet"} explain what the method of substitution is and how it works.</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"bullet"} develop fluency with computing integrals of combinations of powers, sines, cosines, exponentials and logarithms that are solvable by substitution by hand.</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"bullet"} see a graphical understanding of the method of substitution.</p> | <p>[Apply the method of substitution](file:./Scripts/SubstitutionPractice.m)</p><p></p><p>$\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{\\cos\\left(\\ln(t)+1\\right)}{t}\\; dt = \\sin\\left(\\ln(t)+1\\right)+C${"altText":"integral of (cos(ln(t)+1))\/t dt = sin(ln(t)+1)+C"}</p> |
%[text] | [Integration by Parts](file:./Scripts/ByParts.m) | <p>[ByParts.m](file:./Scripts/ByParts.m)</p><p>[![A visual representation of integration by parts. A large purple rectangle missing a small rectangle in the lower left corner is divided into two areas labeled integral of u dv and integral of v du ](text:image:5137)](file:./Scripts/ByParts.m)</p> | <p>[Visualizing Integration by Parts](file:./Scripts/ByPartsViz.m)</p><p>[![A visual representation of integration by parts showing a large purple rectangle missing a small black rectangle in the lower left corner divided into two areas by a curve from bottom-left to top-right. Below this curve, the area is divided into vertical rectangles. Above this curve, the area is divided into horizontal rectangles.](text:image:6833)](file:./Scripts/ByPartsViz.m)</p> | <p>$\\bullet${"altText":"bullet"} explain what the method of integration by parts is and how it works.</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"bullet"} develop fluency with computing integrals involving powers, sines, cosines, exponentials and logarithms that are solvable by integration by parts by hand.</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"bullet"} see a graphical understanding of the integration by parts formula.</p> | <p>[Apply the method of integration by parts](file:./Scripts/ByPartsPractice.m)</p><p></p><p>[$\\displaystyle \\int y^2e^y\\; dy = y^2e^y-2ye^y+2e^y+C${"altText":"integral of y^2e^y dy = y^2e^y-2ye^y+2e^y+C"}](file:./Scripts/ByPartsPractice.m)</p><p> [$= (y^2-2y+2)e^y+C${"altText":"= (y^2-2y+2)e^y+C"}](<matlab: edit ByPartsPractice.m>)</p> |
%[text:table]
